---
// Hand-authored, derived from private career notes and scrubbed per the site plan.
// Facts come from the public resume; do not paste raw source material here.
import WorkPage from '../../layouts/WorkPage.astro';
import Tradeoff from '../../components/Tradeoff.astro';
---

<WorkPage
  title="AI-Assisted SDLC Workflow"
  oneLiner="A multi-agent pipeline that takes a Jira ticket to a reviewable pull request inside a production CI/CD gate, with the human keeping final authority."
  role="Designer and builder"
  timeframe="FICO Platform · 2026"
  stack={['Multi-agent orchestration', 'CI/CD', 'Jira', 'GitHub']}
  metrics={[
    { value: '2–3', label: 'tickets per week' },
    { value: '9', label: 'services covered' },
    { value: '2', label: 'review layers pre-human' },
    { value: '0', label: 'auto-merges, by design' },
  ]}
  description="A multi-agent AI workflow deployed into FICO's internal CI/CD pipeline, taking Jira tickets to reviewable pull requests."
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    A typical platform ticket demands far more context than code. Before writing
    a line, an engineer has to understand the existing codebase patterns, find
    the relevant config files, Helm charts, and test suites, write the right
    kind of test coverage, and follow conventions that are mostly undocumented.
    On an IAM platform of nine services, each with slightly different
    conventions, that context load is the real barrier: engineers new to the
    platform could not contribute confidently until they had absorbed most of
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The goal was to lower that barrier without lowering the quality bar, and
    specifically without removing human judgment from the loop. This was a
    developer-velocity problem to solve with infrastructure, not with pressure.
  </p>

  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    I designed and deployed a multi-agent workflow that operates on Jira tickets
    end to end, integrated into the team's existing CI/CD pipeline and its
    internal policy-compliance bot. It runs in production, currently processing
    two to three tickets per week across the team.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <div class="diagram">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 740 350" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a Jira ticket flows through a planning agent, execution agent, and review sub-agent with up to two revision cycles, then through the policy bot and a feedback distiller to a human-reviewed pull request">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow4" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor" />
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25">
          <rect x="20" y="30" width="130" height="50" rx="4" />
          <rect x="210" y="30" width="150" height="50" rx="4" />
          <rect x="420" y="30" width="150" height="50" rx="4" />
          <rect x="420" y="140" width="150" height="50" rx="4" />
          <rect x="210" y="140" width="150" height="50" rx="4" />
          <rect x="20" y="250" width="180" height="56" rx="4" />
          <rect x="420" y="250" width="180" height="56" rx="4" />
        </g>
        <g fill="currentColor" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">
          <text x="85" y="60">Jira ticket</text>
          <text x="285" y="53">Planning agent</text>
          <text x="285" y="71" opacity="0.6">context + plan</text>
          <text x="495" y="53">Execution agent</text>
          <text x="495" y="71" opacity="0.6">code, tests, commits</text>
          <text x="495" y="163">Review sub-agent</text>
          <text x="495" y="181" opacity="0.6">≤ 2 revision cycles</text>
          <text x="285" y="163">Policy bot</text>
          <text x="285" y="181" opacity="0.6">static analysis gate</text>
          <text x="110" y="273">Feedback distiller</text>
          <text x="110" y="292" opacity="0.6">bot output → suggestions</text>
          <text x="510" y="273">Human review</text>
          <text x="510" y="292" opacity="0.6">final authority, no auto-merge</text>
        </g>
        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" fill="none" marker-end="url(#arrow4)">
          <line x1="150" y1="55" x2="204" y2="55" />
          <line x1="360" y1="55" x2="414" y2="55" />
          <line x1="495" y1="80" x2="495" y2="134" />
          <line x1="414" y1="165" x2="366" y2="165" />
          <line x1="285" y1="190" x2="285" y2="230" />
          <path d="M 285 230 L 285 240 L 110 240 L 110 244" />
          <line x1="200" y1="278" x2="414" y2="278" />
        </g>
        <g fill="currentColor" font-size="12" opacity="0.6">
          <text x="585" y="110">review</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Two machine review layers run before any human sees the pull request.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    A planning agent reads the ticket and the project context and produces a
    step-by-step plan naming the services, files, and tests involved. An
    execution agent implements the plan, runs the test suites, and commits to a
    feature branch. A review sub-agent then checks the diff for convention
    adherence, missed edge cases, and potential regressions before the PR ever
    exists, cycling back to the execution agent at most twice. The existing
    policy bot runs its static analysis and compliance checks as always, and a
    final distillation step translates its raw findings into actionable
    suggestions, so the human reviewer reads "add a unit test for the
    unauthorized-access case" instead of a rule violation ID.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>

  <Tradeoff decision="Two machine review layers before the human over sending agent output straight to review">
    <span slot="bought">
      The human reviewer's time shifts from mechanical issues (naming,
      formatting, obvious edge cases) to architecture. Most convention-level
      problems die before the PR is opened.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Latency and compute per ticket, and the human sees curated output rather
      than raw signal. The distillation layer has to be trustworthy, because a
      summary that hides a real finding is worse than noise.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="A hard cap of two revision cycles over looping until the review passes clean">
    <span slot="bought">
      Bounded cost and no infinite revision loops. A ticket that cannot
      converge escalates to the human with a summary of what is unresolved,
      which is itself useful signal about the ticket.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      Some PRs reach the human with known open issues. That is deliberate: the
      alternative is an agent burning unbounded cycles on a problem that
      needed human judgment two iterations ago.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <Tradeoff decision="Human keeps final authority over end-to-end automation">
    <span slot="bought">
      Trust and adoption. Nothing merges without a human decision, so the
      team's quality bar and accountability model stay intact, and skeptical
      reviewers could adopt the tool without accepting new risk.
    </span>
    <span slot="cost">
      A throughput ceiling: the pipeline can only move as fast as human review
      capacity. For a platform where a bad merge means an authorization or
      authentication defect, that ceiling is the correct price.
    </span>
  </Tradeoff>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    The honest status: the workflow is in production and deliberately early. It
    processes two to three tickets per week, and the wins so far are observed
    rather than benchmarked. Engineers new to the platform contribute after
    reading a generated plan and PR instead of absorbing nine services'
    conventions up front. Reviewers report spending their attention on
    architecture rather than mechanics, and time from ticket assignment to
    mergeable PR has dropped. Quantified velocity metrics are being collected
    now; I would rather publish observed effects than invent numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
  <p>
    Every ticket currently gets equal agent effort, so a priority-weighted
    scheduler is the first improvement. The second is per-service context
    profiles: the agents learn globally, but conventions differ per service,
    and the review layer would be sharper if each service carried its own
    profile. Longer term, letting the planning agent read the observability
    stack would let it flag performance implications before implementation
    rather than in review.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Related: <a href="/work/authorization-platform/">Authorization Platform</a> ·
    <a href="/work/authentication-platform/">Authentication Platform</a>
  </p>
</WorkPage>
